pert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Maryland remains one of the most Democratic-leaning states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly fifteen points toward Democrats. The incumbent Republican president is term-limited, so both parties will nominate fresh candidates, and no sitting president will be on the ballot to reshape the state's usual pattern. No public polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available for this race yet, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan fundamentals.\n\nRecent headlines point to continued Democratic strength: Governor Wes Moore is running for re-election amid 2028 speculation, and Democrats are advancing a redistricting plan that Republicans are challenging in court. Those signals reinforce the state's lean rather than cut against it. The Republican nominee starts as a clear underdog, but the absence of polling means the margin estimate carries wide uncertainty until surveys arrive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9506, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9102, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9258, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9194, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9194, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9136, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9144, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9103, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9184, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9155, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9262, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.91, "margin": 15.0}]}